A||||12||fiero56||engine removal||||5
Z||000000||fiero56||11-19-1999||08:37 PM||||Guys,<BR>I'm in the middle of dropping my engine and I forgot all about the a/c.  Is it possible to just dissconect the compressor, let it hang, and leave the system pressurised?  I'm in a pickle here already and need some help.  Thanks.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||11-19-1999||08:58 PM||||yep||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||11-19-1999||09:07 PM||||Phil has become a man of few words. Yes, unbolt the compressor from the engine, but leave the hoses connected. Wire it up somewhere out of the way. This is the standard operating procedure. Disconnecting the hoses and allowing the freon to escape into the air is illegal anyway.||||reg||1
Z||000003||RZURCTN||11-19-1999||11:11 PM||||I whole-heartedly agree!  That is what I've done with mine!  As a matter of fact.  The empty battery tray makes a really nice A/C storage compartment while the engine is out.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||11-19-1999||11:22 PM||||I've noticed Phil has been on a one word answer streak lately also. Feeling alright up there?<BR>You guys gave the answer I was looking for.  Haynes said I "must" have it professionally purged... But I didn't feel like freezing my arm or something trying to pull the compressor fully charged. I'll pull it tommorow before I drop the engine. Only other snag is the salve cylinder is really stuck on the steel bradied rubber hose. Hopefully lots of WD40 and medium tapping will help. Other than that it's going pretty good.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 11-20-1999).]||||reg||7
Z||000005||Phil||11-20-1999||07:40 AM||||You don't have to remove the slave from the hose just remove it from the bell housing and let it hang.(Do I get credit for 21 posts now?)||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||11-20-1999||01:43 PM||||Well, I got the car on jackstands this morning, finally. Just about have the a/c compressor out(couple more tugs). Fuel lines are disconnected. I'm getting ready to pull the left suspension out for tranny clearance. <BR>Is it possible to replace just the flexible steel braided hose that goes from the steel line to the slave? I worry about being able to get a good seal between a new flexible line and the regualr steel line. I bought a new slave cylinder and want to be able to put it on.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 11-20-1999).]||||reg||14
Z||000007||fiero56||11-21-1999||06:29 PM||||Well,<BR>I got the cradle down.  I'll lower the rest tommorrow.  I have to remove the right rear suspsension, and the exhaust crossover pipe.  Suspension should be a piece of cake, the left side was.  Exhaust is another story.  I think I'm going to end up buying a dremel and just cut those exhaust bolts.  EGR tube was shot! Must have been for a long time, all kinds of crap in it.  It was broken right under the plenum (plus I broke the braket that holds it to the EGR) anybody  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">. No codes were set from it, and I know the bulb works. Go figure. Well, I hope to get the exhaust off tommorow. ||||reg||7
Z||000008||Carrolles||11-22-1999||12:09 AM||||I'm confused.  Do you have the entire cradle out of the car with the engine, trans, etc?<P>I just replaced the v-6 in my 85SE.  I dropped the entire cradle with engine etc attached.  The entire exhaust system comes out with it.  I disconnected the top of the struts from the towers and left the suspension attached to the cradle.<P>With the entire system on a dolly it was relatively easy to dismantle/remove everything to lift the engine off the trans.<P>I managed to get all the exhaust system off with only breaking off one exhaust manifold stud.||||reg||1
Z||000009||fiero56||11-22-1999||07:53 AM||||The engine is still attached to the crade, but the cradle is tilted down below the frame for clearance when I pull the bellhousing/transmission.  On the 88's the entire rear suspension has to come off separately due to the fact that the '88 has the rear sway bar.  I've never seen the underside of a pre '88 so I don't know everything that would be different, but Ed Parks said to do it this way. To allow clearance for the bellhousing, I have to pull the crossover pipe that runs from both exhaust "manifolds". <p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 11-22-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Phil||11-22-1999||09:08 AM||||I pulled an 88 with suspension, sway bar exhaust etc. intact- disconnected wiring, a/c pump, brakes and then dropped the cradle with struts attached.||||reg||1
Z||000011||fiero56||11-22-1999||10:11 PM||||Well I got the exhaust crossover pipe disconnected tonight.  Ended up having to buy a dremel to cut the bolts.  Now I have to disconnect the stabilizer bar from the right side suspension.  Slave cylinder finally came loose tonight also  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  Now to replace the master cylinder and clutch pedal.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Sage||11-22-1999||10:33 PM||||I'm with Phil.  Just put clutch in 88coupe and took whole thing out together.  Unhooked harness at computer, off firewall, behind battery and pulled with whole assembly.  Had to take long bolts out of trailing arms to get axles out of tranny, and that was after whole thing was on dolly.  Other than that exhaust, suspension and as I said even wiring stayed intact.  Coupe does not have a sway bar though.  Did our 84 last year for motor rebulid and it was same way except for the the a-arm suspension and it too all came out together. On the 84 I left the a/c charged and removed compressor and hung it out of the way but found this to be a pain, so on 88, just disconnected lines to back of compressor and pulled compressor out with cradle assembly.  Will have to recharge system when weather gets hot again. Anyway good luck with your project, I'm sure you'll get it sooner or later, one way or another.  Persistance pays off!<p>[This message has been edited by Sage (edited 11-22-1999).]||||reg||1
